I have with me this afternoon John White  who is the State Superintendent of  Education for the state of Louisiana he  was the deputy Chancellor for the New  York City school system so he's had a  lot of experience with a broad range of  organizations and positions in education  and currently he's the chair of the  Chiefs for change in organization of  state chief officers who are committed  to reform in American education and I'm  very pleased to have the opportunity to  talk with him today about the amazing  developments that have been occurring in  Louisiana Louisiana has always been a  low-performing state but in recent years  under John White's leadership this state  has made more progress than any other  state in the United States according to  the National Assessment of Educational  Progress and it all starts perhaps  with Katrina the great hurricane in New  Orleans because that washed away the  pre-existing school system so John  that's what I'd like to really ask you  about how did it happen that a new  school system was built out of shall I  say the ashes for at least the mud  sludge of the Katrina storm great great  tragedy because we call it a man-made  tragedy in New Orleans but prior to  Katrina New Orleans by any measure was  the lowest performing urban school  system in the country it was the second  lowest performing school system and the  second lowest performing state in  America when you have to vacate your  entire city for weeks and months and  sometimes even years for many of our  people you have no students when you  have no students you have no revenue for  a school board and when that happened  the school board in New Orleans in 2005  tragically severed its contract with its  teachers  and the state stepped in and said well  we will then take over the governance of  every school in New Orleans which is  struggling academically and that was  nearly 90 percent of the schools over  time the state transitioned those  schools from being run by the state to  being operated by independent charter  school organizations that took many many  years but it took a decade and now we  are where we are with a system that is  almost exclusively independent charter  schools well actually you had a little  help I think that's what I've heard and  maybe you could tell me where there's  some outside resources that became  available simply because the tragedy  captured national attention and the  Foundation's come in question I mean New  Orleans is a city that is at the heart  of the American identity it's a city  that whenever you mentioned people have  smiles across their faces and it's also  a city of great poverty and  philanthropic interests know that from  across the country as well as from  within Louisiana also people in a range  of issues from blight and urban  development issues all the way to  education and health issues came to  Louisiana I count myself as one of them  to help with what I think of as a  reformation but not just of a school  system since a storm really of tragedies  that have befallen low-income people in  Louisiana and in the south for centuries  and that's what our project is about but  no question about it both people from  the inside and people from the outside  were part of this isn't young people  came to didn't they didn't young  teachers from around the country feel it  was sort of their moral obligation to go  to New Orleans and help out during this  time of stress yeah I think there was a  missionary feel to it certainly I view  it as a moral calling at the same time  it also gave rise to old generation of  New Orleanians who had as is the case in  New York or anywhere else existed within  a school board establishment that's very  hard to be creative within and very hard  to be upwardly mobile in you know so  there are a lot of veteran New Orleans  educators who are now able to run  schools that they always wanted to run  and they're able to do it in a charter  school system where they work for a  volunteer board rather than for an  elected board and serpent's a lot of  internal energy as well as bringing in  external energy yeah no question about  it and and you know over the last ten  years  we've been finding our way to how do you  give life to local voices how do you  recruit the right outside voices and how  when you recruit outside voices do you  make sure that the things that are  important to us in New Orleans music for  example in the arts incredibly important  to the identity of people in New Orleans  and to their upbringing how do you make  sure that you never lose those things  even when you have outside talent and  the federal government helped with some  building of schools was that part of the  part of the assets doesn't were able to  work without heard you know every child  now is in a renovated or a fully  reconstructed building you know some  amazing buildings but the school where  Ruby Bridges went to school in New  Orleans we have totally rebuilt  preserved the facade and preserved her  classroom and it's amazing the resources  that came into New Orleans after that  great tragedy and of course those are  opportunities for the next generation of  kids as much as there were tragedies for  many of the kids who were there we also  benefited from an investment innovation  at the federal government the Obama  administration rather than the Bush  administration in this case made to grow  high performing charter organizations  and you know so we've done some good  things we've certainly got had a lot of  help along the way it's not resources  that every city has at their disposal  but it's also fair to say the principles  of it independent schools recruiting  talent those are things that anybody can  do well is there any good evidence that  all of this has paid off that students  are actually learning more today than  they had been in the days before all of  this renovation took place one of the  things were also blessed with is it  wasn't just educators but it was  political scientists and economists who  came to Louisiana they wanted to study  this stuff too in fact Tulane has  started a center run by an economist  named Doug Harris and economists got  together and studied the effects in  multiple ways of what's happened in  Louisiana and in New Orleans  specifically and had concluded that  statistically no school system that  those researchers are aware of has  demonstrated the effect on student  achievement in such a short period of  time than having a while in schools I do  want to be clear though we have a long  long way to go you know New Orleans has  gone from the bottom of the state in  Louisiana to around the median in the  state of Louisiana which given its  poverty rate another thing  incredible thing but it's not nearly at  the top of our state and our state still  performs relatively low in the national  ranking so we have a long long way to go  well Louisiana is also enacted a school  voucher program so it's a statewide  school voucher program I think it has  its component in New Orleans but  elsewhere throughout the state how has  that worked out so it's shown a lot of  good positive effects it's shown  positive effects in terms of greater  integration of schools it's shown  positive effects in terms of parent  approval it's begun to show positive  effects in initial research in terms of  the effect of having greater levels of  competition so kids in other schools in  proximity to the voucher schools are  doing well and we'll have research  coming out soon that I think will  demonstrate that the voucher schools  right now are performing on a par with  schools in the rest of the system that  said you know for me it's not about  whether it's a private school a charter  school or a traditional public school it  just is it a good school my viewers we  should measure them all give parents  access to all of them and and that the  system will be better for parents having  that choice provided that we can  validate that there's good work going on  in their schools well the overall system  in Louisiana is moving forward what do  you attribute that to it's a simple  formula you know this stuff is not is  not rocket science but it's actually  having access to early childhood  education it's having benchmark  standards and assessments in curriculum  and curriculum matters I have to say  that it's having prepared teachers who  are prepared in the classroom not in the  ivory tower a pathway to prosperity for  every graduate and revitalizing our  career technical education system and  then finally not being afraid to  intervene you know when things are  really going off the rails and typically  in our country that affects low-income  kids of color you can't be afraid to  intervene and so we're looking at Baton  Rouge and doing work in Shreveport and  other cities where there are high  concentrations of struggling schools and  low-income neighborhoods that's not  something that we can tolerate and you  have to be willing to do something about  it so you're the head of keep for change  achieves for change which is trying to  introduce reforms throughout the country  working together with other people who  are like you the head of the educational  system in that stage so how can you  export  you're learning in Louisiana how does it  work when you are working with an  organization like that  well Chase for change is a group of  there's a handful of us who are States  Chiefs in Mississippi New Mexico Nevada  Massachusetts the Washington DC State  Superintendent so we've got a good mix  of State Chiefs we've also got district  Chiefs and you know you in our system  and the fragmented American education  system you have to have both on boards  you can't have the state in the district  working consistently at odds with one  another so we have huge district  superintendents that are doing amazing  things from California all the way to  Florida in our group too and you know  where we have formed working groups to  take on what we think are the greatest  challenges coming up one of them is how  should schools be governed in times when  the current governing structure is not  working what should be done and I think  that's an incredibly powerful question  given everything that we've tried that's  worked based on research over the last  10 years under No Child Left Behind and  race to the top but also there's a lot  that hasn't worked and there's a lot of  wasted money and wasted activity and so  that's one example of how we can work  together to say this is what we're  committed to doing together well so let  me be specific about that how about the  New Orleans model where every school is  a school of choice is that something  that should be put into place in other  big cities around the country and we're  experiencing this of course in  Massachusetts as we have this dialogue  that there is a referendum on the ballot  regarding our charter schools a good  thing and the research in Massachusetts  would demonstrate without question that  they are in Louisiana it would  demonstrate without question that there  are and there are other places that our  charter schools have really struggled so  it's not just is an all charter school  system a good thing I mean let's be  honest look at Detroit it's not working  at the scale that it needs to work at it  the quality it needs to work at so can  it be done well yes if there's a plan to  have talent to have accountability to  have the right governance model to offer  options to families and to intervene  when schools don't do well you know if  you have those things in place it can  work and there's research now in  Massachusetts and Louisiana and  elsewhere to demonstrate that it can  work but it's not just charter schools  it's having a plan for who runs them and  what to do  that'll work no magic bullet is going to  solve the problem of embarrassing  occasion no question John this has been  a great opportunity for me to discuss  our schools and where we need to go  thank you very much for joining me today  one for me as well thanks Paul thanks  you
 

